{
  "result": "At the edge of town stood a library that opened only when it rained. Nobody knew who owned it, although everyone had a theory. Some said a retired sailor had built it to keep his maps dry; others insisted the building itself was a cloud that had decided to settle down.\n\nMira first found it on a Tuesday when the sky turned the color of an old spoon. She had missed her bus, lost a glove, and argued with her best friend before breakfast. By noon, she was convinced the day had singled her out for punishment.\n\nInside the library, a woman in yellow boots handed her a towel and pointed toward a shelf labeled UNFINISHED THINGS. Each book contained a story that had stopped just before someone made a difficult choice. In one, a gardener hesitated to plant an unfamiliar seed. In another, a brother stood outside a door, rehearsing an apology.\n\nMira read until the rain softened. Then she asked what happened next.\n\n\u201cThat depends,\u201d said the woman, \u201con who picks up the book.\u201d\n\nOutside, the clouds were breaking apart. Mira borrowed a pencil, wrote a short note to her friend, and left it at her door. It was not a perfect apology, but it was honest. By evening, a reply appeared beneath her own door: a drawing of two mismatched gloves, holding hands.\n\nThe next morning was bright and clear. The library had vanished, but Mira kept the pencil, just in case another story needed a beginning.",
